 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Rich can pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json`. The output is re-serialized as multi-line JSON, indented by 4 spaces by default (set the ``indent`` argument to change this), and is syntax highlighted unless ``highlight=False`` is passed::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output can be produced by passing a :class:`~rich.json.JSON` renderable to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. The renderable disables word wrapping on its text, so printing or logging it does not wrap lines. Under default print options, lines wider than the console are still cropped to the console width; pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to keep full lines.
+
+You can also import ``print_json`` from the package root, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+Rich also provides a command-line entry point which pretty prints the contents of a JSON file. Run it with the module and a path::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
